Ladybird resolves macOS Control-key bindings in the UI and carries the
resulting commands with the original keyboard event. Gecko records
AppKit selectors in nsCocoaUtils::GetCommandsFromKeyEvent(), then maps
them in NativeKeyBindings::AppendEditCommandsForSelector(). WebKit’s
WebViewImpl::collectKeyboardLayoutCommandsForEvent() records AppKit
commands. Chromium records selectors in its native Cocoa view method,
RenderWidgetHostViewCocoa::doCommandBySelector:.

Ladybird executes deletion after keyboard and beforeinput cancellation
checks, using word or paragraph-boundary ranges. WebKit routes
executeDeleteWordBackward() and executeDeleteToEndOfParagraph() through
Editor::deleteWithDirection(). Blink routes ExecuteDeleteWordBackward()
and ExecuteDeleteToEndOfParagraph() through DeleteWithDirection(). Both
delete the separator when paragraph-end deletion starts there.

Ladybird reports paragraph-end deletion as deleteHardLineForward,
matching Blink and WebKit. Gecko computes the hard-line range with
CreateRangeExtendedToNextHardLineBreak(), but maps the action through
eDeleteToEndOfSoftLine and reports deleteSoftLineForward. Ladybird uses
the hard-line input type because its range ends at a paragraph boundary.

Ladybird resolves physical line edges from shaped text rather than block
direction alone. Blink uses SelectionModifyDirection::kLeft or kRight
with TextGranularity::kLineBoundary in MoveCommands. WebKit uses
SelectionDirection::Left or Right with TextGranularity::LineBoundary in
its editing commands. Gecko uses visual movement and the edge frame bidi
level in nsFrameSelection::MoveCaret().

Ladybird keeps physical line-edge navigation inside the editing host.
Blink bounds StartOfLine() and EndOfLine() with HighestEditableRoot().
WebKit line-boundary helpers call honorEditingBoundaryAtOrBefore() or
honorEditingBoundaryAtOrAfter(). Gecko passes the ancestor returned by
GetAncestorLimiterForCaretMove() into PeekOffsetForCaretMove().
